# Copy this file to .env and fill in your real values.
# .env is git-ignored and never committed.

# OpenWeatherMap API key (free tier works for current weather + cloud tiles)
OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=your_owm_key_here

# Google Calendar iCal URL (from Google Calendar → Settings → Private address in iCal format)
CALENDAR_ICAL_URL=https://calendar.google.com/calendar/ical/YOUR_CALENDAR_ID/private-TOKEN/basic.ics

# Default location for weather (skips external geolocation API entirely — recommended)
# Find your coordinates at https://www.latlong.net/
DEFAULT_LAT=25.68
DEFAULT_LON=-100.31

# Optional: change the port the server listens on (default 3001)
# PORT=3001

# Optional: which assistant profile to boot into (name, personality, and voice).
# One of: martin (default), jarvis, touchsphere, miku, merlin, jess. The user can
# switch this any time in Settings → Assistant; this env only seeds the default.
# Profiles are defined in server/src/config/assistant.ts (persona + voice) and
# client/src/config/assistant.ts (name + wake words + avatar).
# ASSISTANT_NAME=martin

# ── Running the AI locally ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every AI tool in this app can run on your own hardware; this block is the
# whole configuration for that, in one place. Each line below is explained in
# its own section further down.
#
#   OLLAMA_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:11434     # chat (the assistant, its tools)
#   OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3:8b                    # a model that makes tool calls properly
#   OLLAMA_GUIDE_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:11434    # game-guide writer (defaults to OLLAMA_URL)
#   OLLAMA_IMAGE_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:11434    # prompt improver, vision, planner (same)
#   OLLAMA_VISION_MODEL=gemma4               # the picture-side model must have vision
#   WHISPER_URL=http://whisper:8000          # speech-to-text — the `whisper` compose service
#   KOKORO_URL=http://kokoro:8880            # speech — the `kokoro` compose service
#   RVC_URL=http://rvc:5050                  # Miku's voice conversion
#   TTS_PROVIDER=local                       # Kokoro before ElevenLabs (cloud kept as fallback)
#   COMFYUI_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:8188     # image generation
#   SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080          # web search — the `searxng` compose service
#   SEARCH_PREFER_LOCAL=1                    # SearXNG before Ollama's hosted search
#
# Then start the services. The voice ones run in EITHER place — on this box:
#       docker compose --profile local-voice up -d      (whisper + kokoro + rvc)
# or on the GPU box, with the URLs above pointed at it (this is also where
# ComfyUI lives, there being no CPU twin for it):
#       docker compose -f docker-compose.voice.yml --profile gpu up -d
# and, on this box either way:
#       docker compose up -d searxng
# Check it with scripts/check-local-ai.sh, which probes every service and
# says which are answering — and which are local. Settings → Debug shows the
# same chains ("Speech-to-text: whisper (local) → elevenlabs (cloud)").
#
# What stays in the cloud, and why — the two things with no local twin:
#   • Reading a page that refuses this server (every Fandom wiki answers a bare
#     fetch with a Cloudflare challenge). Only Ollama's hosted web_fetch gets
#     through, and only with OLLAMA_API_KEY set. Leave the key set for that
#     alone; with SEARCH_PREFER_LOCAL=1 it is asked nothing else until SearXNG
#     comes up empty. Without the key those pages are simply unreadable.
#   • ElevenLabs' particular character voices. Kokoro speaks perfectly well —
#     TTS_PROVIDER=local makes it the voice — but its stock voices are not the
#     ones the assistants were written for, which is taste, not capability.
#     Miku's voice is local either way (kokoro → rvc).
# The cloud keys can all be left set: each is only ever the fallback once the
# local service is configured, and is never the first thing asked.
#
# The chat model itself. Local by default (host.docker.internal:11434 — Ollama on
# this docker host); Ollama's hosted service works with OLLAMA_URL=https://ollama.com
# plus OLLAMA_API_KEY, and OLLAMA_FALLBACK_URL/_MODEL name a second box tried when
# the first answers 429/5xx or not at all (a local model behind a cloud one, or
# the reverse). OLLAMA_NUM_CTX (32768) must cover the ~14k tokens of system
# prompt and tool schemas — Ollama's 4096 default silently discards them.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3:8b
# OLLAMA_API_KEY=
# OLLAMA_FALLBACK_URL=
# OLLAMA_FALLBACK_MODEL=
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=32768

# ── Speech-to-text ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two providers, tried in this order — the local one first, the cloud one as
# its fallback — so a box with a Whisper container hears with the internet
# down, and a cold or missing container costs one round trip, never the
# utterance. With NEITHER set, voice input is disabled (the assistant can
# still be typed to).
#
# WHISPER_URL — any server that speaks OpenAI's POST /v1/audio/transcriptions:
#   • the `whisper` service in docker-compose.yml (speaches: faster-whisper
#     behind that API, CPU image, also on arm64). Start it with
#       docker compose --profile local-voice up -d
#     and keep the default below. Or run it on the GPU box through
#     docker-compose.voice.yml (--profile gpu uses the CUDA image) and point
#     this at it: WHISPER_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:8000
#   • faster-whisper-server, LocalAI, vLLM — same path, same fields.
#   • whisper.cpp's own server: start it with --convert (the browser sends
#     webm/opus, and without that flag it accepts only 16 kHz WAV) and set
#     WHISPER_PATH=/inference.
WHISPER_URL=http://whisper:8000
# The `model` field of the request. speaches downloads a Hugging Face repo by
# this name on first use (kept in the whisper-models volume): `small` is the
# right size for a 4-core CPU box, ~1–2 s a sentence once warm; on a GPU box
# Systran/faster-distil-whisper-large-v3 mishears far less and is still fast.
# whisper.cpp ignores the field (its model is a command-line flag).
# WHISPER_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small
# WHISPER_PATH=/v1/audio/transcriptions
# One transcription's budget. Generous because the FIRST request after a
# container start loads the model inside it; the app sends a second of silence
# 15 s after boot to spend that while nobody is talking.
# WHISPER_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# Pin one provider — mainly to prove which is answering. Unset = local first.
# STT_PROVIDER=whisper|elevenlabs
# ElevenLabs Scribe, the cloud fallback, uses ELEVENLABS_API_KEY from the next
# section; ELEVENLABS_STT_MODEL picks its model (scribe_v1).

# ── Text-to-speech ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Providers are tried as a CHAIN per assistant, falling through on failure:
#   Miku:      rvc (kokoro→rvc, local) → elevenlabs → kokoro → espeak
#   everyone:  elevenlabs → kokoro → espeak
# So a dead network, a cold RVC container, or an ElevenLabs 429 costs an
# assistant its preferred voice — never its ability to speak.

# ElevenLabs — the primary voice for every assistant except Miku. The voice IS
# the character here, and these are the voices the characters were written for.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=your_elevenlabs_key_here

# Kokoro — LOCAL neural TTS: no key, no billing, works with the internet down.
# It sits BEHIND ElevenLabs as the fallback — its stock voices don't match the
# characters, but it keeps the dashboard talking when the network or the API key
# is unavailable, which is a much better floor than espeak's robot.
# Voices are per-assistant in server/src/config/assistant.ts (`kokoroVoice`).
#
# WHERE it runs is up to you — the app only ever talks HTTP:
#   • On this box:  keep the defaults below and start the containers with
#                     docker compose --profile local-voice up -d
#   • On a beefier machine (e.g. the one running Ollama — recommended if replies
#     sound slow here): run docker-compose.voice.yml THERE (it has a GPU profile
#     that makes RVC near-instant), then point these at it:
#       KOKORO_URL=http://<llm-box-ip>:8880
#       RVC_URL=http://<llm-box-ip>:5050
KOKORO_URL=http://kokoro:8880
# Optional global override — pins EVERY assistant to one voice. Leave unset to
# let each profile use its own.
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID=eleven_turbo_v2_5

# RVC — LOCAL voice conversion, used for Miku only. RVC doesn't synthesise
# speech, it re-timbres it: Kokoro says the words, RVC turns them into Miku.
# Free, offline, no API key. Runs as its own container (docker/rvc/Dockerfile).
# Put the voice model on the host at ./rvc-models/miku/ (a .pth + optional
# .index) — free Miku RVC models are on Hugging Face. Without the model, the
# conversion fails and Miku falls back to a normal voice; she still talks.
RVC_URL=http://rvc:5050
# The folder name under ./rvc-models to use for Miku.
# RVC_MIKU_MODEL=miku
# Conversion is a second neural pass on CPU, so it needs a longer budget than
# plain TTS. Raise this if you see rvc timeouts on a slow box.
# RVC_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
#
# The two knobs that decide whether she sounds right — and how long it takes:
#   f0method: rmvpe is fast AND accurate. The library's default (harvest) cost
#             ~8s per reply on a CPU box. Don't use it.
#   f0up_key: transpose in SEMITONES. 0 keeps the source voice's pitch and only
#             swaps timbre, which is why an un-transposed Miku sounds like an
#             adult woman with a Miku-ish tone. Raise it until she sits right;
#             4 is a starting point, higher (7–12) gets more character.
# RVC_F0_METHOD=rmvpe
# RVC_F0_UP_KEY=4
# RVC_INDEX_RATE=0.66
# RVC_RMS_MIX_RATE=0.25
# RVC_PROTECT=0.33

# Optional: force ONE provider for every assistant — mainly to pin espeak for a
# fully offline box. Leave unset for the per-assistant behaviour above.
# TTS_PROVIDER=elevenlabs|kokoro|rvc|espeak
# …or `local`, which is not a pin but a reordering: Kokoro speaks for every
# assistant, ElevenLabs is kept BEHIND it as the fallback, and Miku's
# kokoro→rvc pipeline is unchanged. This is the setting for a box that runs
# its AI on its own hardware — pinning `kokoro` would cost Miku her conversion
# and everyone the ElevenLabs fallback, which is "local only", not "local first".
# TTS_PROVIDER=local

# Cover art for the Watch/Play list. Both are optional — without them items fall
# back to a gradient tile generated from the title.
# TMDB (movies + shows): free key from themoviedb.org → Settings → API (v3 key).
TMDB_API_KEY=your_tmdb_v3_key_here
# IGDB (games): create an app at dev.twitch.tv/console/apps — IGDB authenticates
# through Twitch, so you need both the client id and a generated client secret.
IGDB_CLIENT_ID=your_twitch_client_id_here
IGDB_CLIENT_SECRET=your_twitch_client_secret_here

# Video search for the assistant's play_video tool ("show me how to poach an egg").
# Optional: without it the server falls back to parsing youtube.com's own results
# page, which works but is at YouTube's mercy. A key from console.cloud.google.com
# (enable "YouTube Data API v3") makes it reliable — the free quota is far more
# than a dashboard will ever use.
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=your_youtube_data_api_v3_key_here

# Web search for the assistant (web_search, "pull up a page about X") and for
# game-guide research. Two providers, both optional, tried in this order:
#   1. Ollama's hosted search — needs OLLAMA_API_KEY; returns whole pages, but
#      has an HOURLY request limit that one guide's research can exhaust.
#   2. SearXNG — the `searxng` service in docker-compose.yml: keyless, unlimited,
#      snippets only. Start it with `docker compose up -d searxng` and set:
# SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080
# SEARXNG_SECRET=any-random-string
# With neither, the server falls back to a DuckDuckGo scrape (often bot-challenged)
# and Wikipedia's own search.
# SEARCH_PREFER_LOCAL=1 swaps the first two: SearXNG answers first and the hosted
# search is what it falls back to. The hosted search is the better first answer
# on its merits — real ranking, each hit carrying the page's full text — so this
# is a choice about where searches go, not about quality. Reading a page that
# refuses this server (Fandom) still goes through the hosted fetch with the key
# set; that part has no local alternative.
# SEARCH_PREFER_LOCAL=1

# Game guides (Watch/Play list → a game → Guide, or "make me a guide for X").
# Both optional. Guide research reads the game's own wiki through the MediaWiki
# API, so no key is needed for it to work.
# How long one guide-generation model call may take. Much longer than the 30s a
# spoken reply gets: nobody is waiting, and a section of forty steps is a lot of
# tokens for a box that is also driving the voice pipeline.
# OLLAMA_GUIDE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Where the guide model runs (defaults to OLLAMA_URL). Point it at the GPU box so
# a guide's dozen calls never spend the cloud model's usage limit:
# OLLAMA_GUIDE_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:11434
# OLLAMA_GUIDE_MODEL=gemma4
# Gap between scraped web searches, used only when the wiki and Wikipedia paths
# come up empty. DuckDuckGo answers a burst of queries with a challenge page, and
# one guide makes a dozen searches — raise this if research keeps coming back dry.
# GUIDE_SEARCH_GAP_MS=1500

# Image prompt improver (Draw panel -> "Improve my prompt", Settings -> Drawing).
# The model that rewrites what you type into a prompt for the picture model that
# is about to draw it. Its own setting rather than the chat model because nobody
# is waiting on this the way they wait on a spoken reply -- it happens inside a
# render that already takes a minute -- so a slower, stronger model here costs
# nothing you can perceive. Defaults to OLLAMA_MODEL.
# OLLAMA_IMAGE_MODEL=
# Where the picture-side models run — the improver, the vision composer, the
# region finder and the edit planner. Defaults to OLLAMA_URL. Point it at the
# GPU box when chat is on Ollama's cloud service, so the cloud's usage limit
# is spent on conversation and a prompt rewrite never waits on it:
# OLLAMA_IMAGE_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:11434
# How long that one call may take before the render goes ahead with the prompt
# you typed instead. A cold local model can exceed this on its first call, which
# costs you the rewrite and nothing else -- the picture is still drawn.
# OLLAMA_IMAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# The model that LOOKS at a picture before it is redrawn. Plain img2img repaints
# from the prompt alone and never sees the original, so "make it night" over a
# picture of a fox draws "make it night" -- this model is shown the picture and
# the change and writes the description of the whole result. Must have vision
# (gemma4 does). Defaults to OLLAMA_IMAGE_MODEL, then OLLAMA_MODEL.
# OLLAMA_VISION_MODEL=

# How long ONE image render may take once the GPU is actually drawing it. This
# does NOT include time spent waiting behind other jobs in ComfyUI's queue --
# that is bounded separately by COMFYUI_QUEUE_WAIT_MS -- because counting queue
# wait against a render's own budget is what made a busy GPU fail every job it
# was given, leaving each abandoned prompt behind to make the queue deeper.
# COMFYUI_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# The outer bound, covering queue wait as well. Generous on purpose: being tenth
# in line is not an error. It exists only so a wedged image server is eventually
# given up on rather than holding the render chain forever.
# COMFYUI_QUEUE_WAIT_MS=3600000

# Notion task tracker (work-mode bottom-right corner)
# Create an integration at https://www.notion.so/my-integrations and share your database(s) with it.
# NOTION_DATABASE_ID is optional — task databases are discovered (any the integration can see with a
# Status or done checkbox). Set it to name the FIRST CHOICE for new tasks; a comma-separated list — the Home task list
# aggregates every listed database. This is only the initial seed: the boards in
# effect are managed on screen under Settings → Notion (add any database by link,
# hide one, star the one new tasks go to), and that choice is saved server-side
# and takes over. Ids may be dashed or bare 32-hex; both are normalised.
NOTION_API_KEY=secret_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
NOTION_DATABASE_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx,yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy

# ── Image generation (ComfyUI) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same arrangement as the voice pipeline: the app only ever talks HTTP, so where
# ComfyUI runs is purely a decision made here. In practice it runs on the box
# with the GPU — docker-compose.voice.yml starts it under the same `gpu` profile
# as Kokoro and RVC:
#
#   On the GPU machine:
#     mkdir -p comfy/models/checkpoints   # drop a .safetensors in
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.voice.yml --profile gpu up -d
#     # then expose it however you reach that box, e.g. over Tailscale:
#     tailscale serve --bg --tcp=8188 tcp://127.0.0.1:8188
#
#   Here:
#     COMFYUI_URL=http://<gpu-box-ip>:8188
#
# UNLIKE TTS, THIS HAS NO FALLBACK. The voice chain always has espeak
# underneath it, so a dead GPU box costs an assistant her voice, not her ability
# to speak. There is no espeak for pictures — with COMFYUI_URL unset or the box
# switched off, the assistant is never offered the generate_image tool at all,
# so she says she can't draw rather than promising a picture that never arrives.
# COMFYUI_URL=http://100.98.235.63:8188

# Which checkpoint to render with — the DEFAULT only. The live choice is
# whatever is picked under "Style" in the Draw widget, persisted to
# image-model.json in $CACHE_DIR, and it applies to spoken requests too: picking
# a model means "draw in this style", so generate_image follows it.
# Leave unset to use whatever the workflow was saved with.
# The name must match a file in ComfyUI's models/checkpoints exactly, or every
# render fails with "value not in list: ckpt_name" — which is why the picker
# lists what ComfyUI actually reports rather than letting you type a name.
#
# Install more by dropping .safetensors files on the GPU box under
# ./comfy/models/checkpoints — they appear in the picker with no restart. Anime
# checkpoints (all public on Hugging Face, ~6.5 GB each):
#   cagliostrolab/animagine-xl-4.0  → animagine-xl-4.0.safetensors
#   cagliostrolab/animagine-xl-3.1  → animagine-xl-3.1.safetensors
# COMFYUI_MODEL=sd_xl_base_1.0.safetensors

# Your own workflow, as ComfyUI's "Save (API Format)" export — NOT the ordinary
# Save, which writes a different shape and is rejected with a bare 400. Defaults
# to $CACHE_DIR/workflows/txt2img.json (i.e. on the Docker volume, so it survives
# a rebuild); with no file there, a built-in txt2img graph is used.
#
# The prompt, size and seed are patched in by NODE CLASS, not by node id, so an
# export of your own keeps working: the sampler is found by class, and the two
# CLIPTextEncode boxes are told apart by following the sampler's own
# positive/negative links. The graph does need a SaveImage node — a
# PreviewImage-only workflow renders into temp and leaves nothing to fetch.
# COMFYUI_WORKFLOW=/data/cache/workflows/txt2img.json

# Default frame size. The assistant picks portrait/landscape/square per request;
# these are only the fallback. The kiosk screen is 720×1280, so tall fits best.
# COMFYUI_WIDTH=768
# COMFYUI_HEIGHT=768
# Override the workflow's step count. Unset = whatever the workflow says.
# This is only the fallback: the Quality row in the Draw widget (draft/standard/
# high = 12/26/44 steps) is the live control, persisted to image-quality.json.
# COMFYUI_STEPS=
#
# ANIMA BASE V1 is offered in the Style picker as a WORKFLOW style, not a
# checkpoint — it's three files behind three loader nodes. Install on the GPU box:
#   B=https://huggingface.co/circlestone-labs/Anima/resolve/main/split_files
#   curl -L -o comfy/models/diffusion_models/anima-base-v1.0.safetensors $B/diffusion_models/anima-base-v1.0.safetensors
#   curl -L -o comfy/models/text_encoders/qwen_3_06b_base.safetensors     $B/text_encoders/qwen_3_06b_base.safetensors
#   curl -L -o comfy/models/vae/qwen_image_vae.safetensors                $B/vae/qwen_image_vae.safetensors
#
# OPTIONAL, 66 MB: Anima's own inpainting patch — a ControlNet-LLLite by
# kohya-ss, repackaged for ComfyUI by Comfy-Org, and the nearest thing to an
# "Anima Edit" there is (checked 2026-09-10: circlestone-labs publishes no
# editor). With it on the box, "Just a part" on any Anima style paints the
# marked part knowing what surrounds it (core ComfyUI's ModelPatchLoader +
# AnimaLLLiteApply, no node pack); without it the masked edit still works the
# generic way, and the picture's details say which happened.
#   mkdir -p comfy/models/model_patches
#   curl -L -o comfy/models/model_patches/anima-lllite-inpainting-v2.safetensors https://huggingface.co/Comfy-Org/Anima-LLLite/resolve/main/model_patches/anima-lllite-inpainting-v2.safetensors
#
# ANIMA 2.9B PREVIEW V1 (Gazingstars123, community) — Anima Base with twelve
# more transformer layers trained on 1.7M further samples (knowledge cutoff
# July 2026). Same text encoder and VAE as above; ComfyUI >= 0.33.1 loads it
# natively. One 5.8 GB file, and the style appears in the picker once it is there:
#   curl -L -o comfy/models/diffusion_models/Anima-2.9B-preview-v1.safetensors https://huggingface.co/Gazingstars123/Anima-2.9B/resolve/main/Anima-2.9B-preview-v1.safetensors
# COMFYUI_NEGATIVE=text, watermark, signature, blurry, lowres, deformed, extra limbs
#
# A whole render, not one HTTP call: queue wait + checkpoint load + sampling.
# The first render after the container starts pays 20–40s of model load.
# COMFYUI_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
#
# How many generated images to keep on the volume. Unlike cover art — bounded by
# the length of the Watch/Play list — these grow without limit, and the volume
# lives on the Pi. Oldest are deleted first.
# IMAGE_MAX_STORED=60

# ── Media stack (Plex / Sonarr / Radarr / Bazarr / Seerr / qBittorrent) ──────
# The top-left corner and the play_media / request_media / download_status /
# media_languages voice tools. EVERYTHING hangs off Plex: with MEDIA_PLEX_URL
# unset the corner says "Not set up" and none of the tools are offered. The
# rest are optional and each unlocks one feature — Seerr the Requests tab and
# request_media, qBittorrent the Downloads tab with pause/resume (Sonarr/Radarr
# alone still show their download queue, read-only), Bazarr the "still looking
# for subtitles in…" line on a film or show.
#
# URLs are as the app CONTAINER reaches them: on the same docker host that is
# the bridge gateway (172.18.0.1 by default, `docker network inspect bridge`),
# elsewhere a LAN or tailnet address. scripts/collect-media-env.py, run on the
# media box, pulls every key out of each service's own config and appends the
# lot — it prints which keys it found, never the values.
# MEDIA_PLEX_URL=http://172.18.0.1:32400
# MEDIA_PLEX_TOKEN=          # PlexOnlineToken in Preferences.xml, or Settings → Troubleshooting → Get Info on any item
# MEDIA_SONARR_URL=http://172.18.0.1:8989
# MEDIA_SONARR_KEY=          # Settings → General → API Key
# MEDIA_RADARR_URL=http://172.18.0.1:7878
# MEDIA_RADARR_KEY=
# MEDIA_BAZARR_URL=http://172.18.0.1:6767
# MEDIA_BAZARR_KEY=
# MEDIA_SEERR_URL=http://172.18.0.1:5055
# MEDIA_SEERR_KEY=           # Settings → General → API Key (Overseerr/Jellyseerr/Seerr)
# MEDIA_QBIT_URL=http://172.18.0.1:8080
# MEDIA_QBIT_USER=
# MEDIA_QBIT_PASS=

# ── Server updates (Settings → Server) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Lets the kiosk update the machine the dashboard runs on: apt packages,
# firmware (fwupd), Tailscale, the other docker stacks, the dashboard itself,
# and reboot. The container reaches the host over SSH with a key it generates
# on its own volume; on the host that key can run ONE root-owned script with a
# fixed list of verbs and nothing else. Setup, on the host, as the user who
# owns the docker stacks:
#     sudo bash scripts/host/install.sh '<the public key shown in Settings → Server>'
# Then set this to that user @ the docker bridge gateway (the host's own
# address from inside the compose network — the installer prints the line).
# Unset = the tab does not exist.
# HOST_UPDATE_SSH=loklo@172.18.0.1

# ── Desk presence sensor (HC-SR04 on the Raspberry Pi) ───────────────────────
# scripts/presence/ on the Pi reports whether someone is in front of the kiosk
# to POST /api/presence; the screen dims after a while away (Settings →
# Hardware) and the assistant is told. Optional shared secret — set it here
# and pass it to scripts/presence/install.sh, and forged reports are refused.
# PRESENCE_TOKEN=
